// src/v4/extdeps/formats/spice.dag
//
// Scope: SPICE netlist model — direction-agnostic. Analog circuit
//        description: components, nodes, .model cards, directives. A flat
//        component + connection graph — NO control flow.
// Anchor: "SPICE3 Version 3f5 User's Guide" (Quarles et al., UC Berkeley
//         ERL, 1993) — the canonical versioned netlist-format spec the
//         worker models against; the ngspice User's Manual (pin a
//         concrete release, e.g. ngspice-44) is the maintained versioned
//         reference. NOT Wikipedia — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SPICE
//         is accessible context only.
// Stress (T-4.10 — a B2-OMNI falsification probe): SPICE is **Shape B**
//   (a data format, per L-1) — it is NOT a `LanguageModel` and never
//   touches the compiler-language (Shape A) ingest boundary. The probe
//   tests the **Shape B format data-model** abstraction only: is it
//   ACTUALLY general, or secretly programming-language-shaped? A netlist
//   has no control flow — it is components plus a connection graph. If
//   the format data-model cannot represent a netlist without contortion,
//   that is a substrate-design signal to surface NOW
//   (STOP-and-escalate), not after it is load-bearing.
// Clear win: ONE `.dag` circuit declaration → a SPICE netlist that
//   simulates (omni-emission reaches analog hardware).
//
// Owns:
//   - SpiceNetlist { title: String, body: List<DeckStatement> } —
//     well-formed by construction; malformed input is a Diagnostic,
//     never a partial parse (same fail-closed discipline as csv.dag's
//     rectangularity). The deck body is a single ordered list of
//     `DeckStatement`s (one of Component / ModelCard /
//     SubcircuitDefinition / Directive). Round-trip scope (PR #3168
//     codex review): TOP-LEVEL source-order interleaving of these
//     four statement kinds round-trips through `parse ∘ emit`.
//     LIFTED nested `.SUBCKT` definitions preserve their parent
//     identity (via `parent_scope`) but NOT their original within-
//     parent positional offset — a documented secondary fidelity
//     concession tracked as 🟡 YELLOW debt with a named consumer
//     trigger (see the LIFTED NESTED `.SUBCKT` POSITION YELLOW note
//     in the SpiceNetlist declaration's header). The contract
//     promised here is therefore "top-level interleave round-trip" +
//     "nested-SUBCKT parent-identity preservation", not "byte-exact
//     deck round-trip". The original scaffold Owns line read
//     `{components, nodes, models, directives}`; the `nodes` field
//     is dropped per Practice 7 (the set of referenced nodes is a
//     projection over component terminals — a parallel `nodes:
//     List<NodeRef>` field would be a second authority, cost-of-
//     change ≥ 2; INVARIANTS P2). Node-STATE declarations (`.IC` /
//     `.NODESET`) are NOT lost — they live as `Directive` variants
//     where they belong (variadic-card cardinality preserved by
//     `NodeVoltageBindings` non-empty carrier). `SubcircuitDefinition`
//     is a first-class deck statement (`DeckSubcircuit` variant),
//     replacing the prior bucketed `subcircuits` field; nested
//     definitions are lifted to top-level with `parent_scope` carrying
//     lexical identity. See the modeling-notes section below for the
//     full reasoning.
//   - parse(String) -> Result<SpiceNetlist, Diagnostic>      (declared
//     signature; the parse body is pipeline-stage work — T-6/T-7 — and
//     does not live in this format-model file, matching the sibling
//     csv/json/yaml format-scaffolds.)
//   - emit(SpiceNetlist) -> String   (Shape B: a `.dag` program walks
//     typed values to produce the netlist; not a compiler render target.
//     Signature only; body deferred for the same reason as `parse`.)
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag — the `Symbol` opaque-identity carrier (K-1).
//     Symbol is conceptually substrate-ambient per node.dag's K-1
//     header note, but the v2 bridge parser requires an explicit
//     import to bring Symbol into the module's resolution scope; the
//     import is a v2-bridge necessity, not a model-level dependency.
//     The `Node` type itself is deliberately NOT imported: this is a
//     Shape-B data format, not a `LanguageModel` — the probe's premise.
//   - std/diagnostic.dag — the fail-closed Diagnostic carrier for
//     malformed input (cited in prose only at this layer, matching
//     sibling format scaffolds — `json.dag`, `csv.dag`, etc. —
//     because parse/emit fn bodies are pipeline-stage work (T-6/T-7)
//     and do not live in this format-model file; the actual
//     `import v4.std.diagnostic { Diagnostic }` lands when those
//     bodies do).
//   (String, Int, List are kernel-ambient per STRUCTURE.md — none is
//   imported. The scaffold-header `Consumes` line originally read
//   `std/node.dag, std/diagnostic.dag, std/collection.dag,
//   std/primitive.dag`; `std/primitive.dag` was deleted by PR #3152
//   (STRUCTURE.md §"Scalar/numeric concept decomposition"); the
//   diagnostic citation moved to prose-only to match sibling format
//   scaffolds; `std/collection.dag` is not consumed because `List` is
//   kernel-ambient and no modeled collection facts are used here.
//   `std/node.dag` is retained for the Symbol bring-in. The line is
//   corrected to actual modeled dependencies — a stale citation from
//   the primitive.dag decomposition is mechanical cleanup, not a
//   contract revision; the Scope/Owns/Stress/Clear-win contract above
//   stands. Header Consumes change pending operator ratification of
//   the systemic scaffold-Consumes reconciliation (T-4 mgr synthesis
//   msg_1fddb75c) — consistent with ptx #3170 and the verilog probe;
//   operator ratifies all per-file Consumes strikes uniformly.)
//
// Status: T-4.10 modeled 2026-05-16 (smart-raven-655). The I/O contract
// in this header is immutable for downstream workers. Splitting this
// file requires explicit operator ratification (substrate extension =
// stop signal).


module v4.extdeps.formats.spice


import v4.std.node { Symbol }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// THE PROBE'S FINDING (stated up front so reviewers can verify).
// SPICE3 is a flat component + connection-graph data format with no
// control flow, no recursion-in-execution, no algebraic carrier. The
// substrate's 6 type connectives + Conj/Disj surface express it
// directly: a `SpiceNetlist` is a `Conj` of lists of `Conj`/`Disj`
// statements. No 6th L1 behavior is required — components do not
// COMPUTE, they DECLARE a topology that an external simulator solves.
// No 7th type connective is required — every netlist element decomposes
// into the existing connectives. The B2-OMNI O(N+M) claim is upheld for
// this target: adding SPICE costs one file (this file), and that file
// does not perturb the substrate. The substrate-design signal the probe
// was authored to surface (a contortion in the connectives or the
// behavior set) is ABSENT.
//
// PROBE LIMITS (honest framing — what this file does NOT validate).
// The B2-OMNI claim covers the format DATA-MODEL. It does NOT validate:
//   - that an external SPICE simulator can be DRIVEN from `emit`'s
//     output (the "clear win" in the header — that requires invoking a
//     simulator process; an `extdeps/process.dag` consumer that
//     coordinates with the emitted netlist is a separate boundary
//     concern, not modeled here).
//   - that SPICE's NUMERIC sub-grammar (suffix-units `1k`/`1u`/`100p`,
//     scientific notation, parameter expressions) round-trips through
//     parse/emit faithfully (the numeric sub-format is itself a Shape-B
//     concern; `SpiceValue` here carries the lexeme verbatim — see
//     `SpiceValue` below — so `parse∘emit` is identity on the lexical
//     surface and the numeric semantics is the simulator's concern, not
//     this file's. A future probe may model the SPICE numeric sub-
//     grammar; that is not in T-4.10's scope.)
//
// FIDELITY SCOPE (PR #3168 codex REQUEST_CHANGES, 2026-05-16). Two
// distinct deliverables for this file, EXPLICITLY DISTINGUISHED so the
// authority claim and the coverage state do not contradict each other:
//   (1) SHAPE DELIVERABLE — the substrate-design decisions the T-4.10
//       probe tests: closed coproduct over SPICE3 element kinds with
//       per-variant terminal slots (`Component`); typed sum for
//       optional fields rather than `Option<NodeRef>` (`SubstrateSpec`);
//       lexical-scope-preserving SUBCKT lift (`parent_scope: List<
//       Symbol>`); `.END` owned by parse/emit discipline rather than
//       as a data variant; subcircuit bodies type-enforced against
//       control-card containment; well-formed-by-construction at the
//       SHAPE layer. **This is COMPLETE for the T-4.10 probe scope.**
//       The B2-OMNI O(N+M) substrate-validation claim rests on the
//       SHAPE, not on coverage exhaustiveness.
//   (2) SPEC-EXHAUSTIVE COVERAGE — full enumeration of every SPICE3F5
//       element kind, `.MODEL` device kind, analysis/control directive,
//       and per-variant grammar facts (instance parameters, transient
//       source specs, etc.). **This is PARTIAL.** The YELLOW
//       classifications + named triggers on `Component` / `ModelKind` /
//       `Directive` track the gap to spec-exhaustive coverage; each
//       missing-list is kept synchronized with the SPICE3F5 anchor
//       (synchronization-discipline note repeated at each gap site).
// What the file CLAIMS authority for: (1). What it DOES NOT yet
// claim: (2). A valid SPICE3 deck using an omitted element/directive
// kind cannot be represented in the current state — this is bounded,
// documented, and the YELLOW triggers + missing-lists name exactly
// what work closes the gap. **The "T-4.10 probe permits non-
// exhaustive coverage" framing means the SHAPE deliverable did not
// require concurrent spec-exhaustive coverage — it does NOT mean the
// fidelity gap is excusable; the YELLOW triggers track that gap as
// load-bearing follow-up, not as discretion to leave unfilled.** A
// downstream consumer that needs spec-exhaustive coverage is the
// trigger that schedules the per-variant sweeps named below.
//
// WHY NO `nodes: List<NodeRef>` INVENTORY FIELD (Practice 7).
// SPICE does not DECLARE its nodes — they emerge from being referenced
// by component terminals (Quarles 1993 §2: nodes are name-referenced,
// not pre-declared; ground is the conventional Symbol whose spelling is
// "0" — kernel-opaque per K-1, so this file never branches on the
// spelling). A separate `nodes` field would be a parallel representation
// of the projection `union over (component, terminal)` — INVARIANTS P2
// single-authority violation, cost-of-change ≥ 2 (every component-
// edit would force a parallel nodes-edit). The projection lives where
// it is consumed; this file does not materialize it. NODE-STATE
// directives (`.IC v(n) = ...`, `.NODESET v(n) = ...`) are first-class
// `Directive` variants and DO appear — they are the "facts about a
// node" the spec recognizes, separate from the node's existence (which
// is just being referenced).
//
// WHY CLOSED `Component` COPRODUCT (P2 vs Practice 7 tension —
// resolved). The SPICE3 element set is closed by the spec (a versioned
// fact, per the anchor); each element-prefix character (R/C/L/V/I/D/Q/
// M/X/K/T/…) carries a SPECIFIC structural shape (terminal count,
// parameter schema). Two designs were attempted:
//   (a) one record `Component { kind: Symbol, terminals: List<NodeRef>,
//       parameters: List<Parameter> }` projected over a closed
//       `ElementSpec` table (Practice 7). REJECTED: makes illegal
//       arities REPRESENTABLE (a `Resistor` with 3 terminals is a legal
//       Component value, rejected only by a check) — INVARIANTS P2
//       (modeling-discipline.md Practice 2) forbids checked-rather-than-
//       unrepresentable illegal states when the type system can express
//       the constraint. The per-element terminal-count IS expressible:
//       it is a fixed natural per kind.
//   (b) closed `Component` coproduct, one variant per kind, each variant
//       carrying NAMED terminal slots (P2). ADOPTED. Cost-of-change is
//       still 1: adding a new SPICE element kind = adding ONE variant
//       here. There is no PARALLEL representation forced elsewhere (no
//       arity-table, no parameter-schema-table to keep in sync), so
//       Practice 7's cost-of-change test is satisfied. The 5-pattern
//       ledger on the type below explains why this is terminal: the
//       variants are STRUCTURALLY heterogeneous (different named terminal
//       slots; Diode/Bjt/Mosfet additionally carry model name-refs) —
//       they are NOT `F<X>` over a separately-declared set (Practice-4
//       parameterized-family pattern — pattern 5 — fails by inspection
//       of the payloads).
//   The tension was apparent, not real: Practice 7's bite is on parallel
//   representations of a SECOND authority. The coproduct dissolution IS
//   the authority — there is no second table the variants mirror.
//
// CLOSED-SET COVERAGE. The variants enumerated below cover the SPICE3
// core element set (Quarles 1993 §11): R/C/L (passives), V/I
// (independent sources), D/Q/M (semiconductor devices, model-referenced),
// K (mutual inductance), X (subcircuit invocation). Eleven element
// kinds are NOT enumerated in this initial cut — linear controlled
// sources (E/F/G/H), JFET (J), LTRA lossy transmission line (O),
// switches (S/W), lossless transmission line (T), URC distributed RC
// (U), MESFET (Z) — each tracked in the `Component` 🟡 trigger's
// missing-list (the synchronization-discipline section). Per FIDELITY
// SCOPE above, this is the spec-exhaustive-coverage gap (deliverable
// (2)), not the shape deliverable (1); the YELLOW trigger is the
// load-bearing follow-up that closes (2), not discretion to leave
// unfilled. The B2-OMNI finding stands on the shape decisions made
// here; the substrate has been shown to model these element kinds
// without contortion, and the same shape extends mechanically to the
// missing kinds.
//
// SUBCIRCUIT NESTING + LEXICAL SCOPING. SPICE3F5 §3.4 permits nested
// `.SUBCKT` definitions, and the names are LEXICALLY SCOPED to their
// enclosing subcircuit. Two design questions:
//   (i)  Recursive `SubcircuitDefinition` (with `subcircuits: List<
//        SubcircuitDefinition>`) would model the nesting structurally
//        but introduces a self-referential type that is NOT `Node` —
//        node.dag A1 reserves recursion-via-type-self-reference for
//        the substrate root, and adding a domain-carrier recursive
//        type is a substrate-design call that exceeds worker authority.
//   (ii) Flattening to a single top-level list with bare `Symbol`-keyed
//        resolution ERASES the lexical scoping: a `.SUBCKT FOO` defined
//        inside `.SUBCKT BAR` would collide with a top-level `.SUBCKT
//        FOO` as competing authorities (P1 / P2 violation — PR #3168
//        codex review surfaced this).
// The current shape resolves both: nested `.SUBCKT name (...)` forms
// are lifted to the top-level list by the parser BUT each
// `SubcircuitDefinition` carries a `parent_scope: List<Symbol>`
// (outermost-first, list-depth = nesting-depth) that preserves the
// lexical-scope identity. Two same-named subcircuits in different
// parents now have DISTINCT `(name, parent_scope)` identities at the
// type layer; `SubcircuitCall` resolution is positional + walks up
// the scope chain per SPICE3 §3.4 shadowing. See the
// `SubcircuitDefinition` declaration's header for the full rationale.
// `List<Symbol>` is the kernel-ambient flat carrier that captures the
// scoping faithfully without introducing a recursive carrier type.
//
// EVERY COPRODUCT BELOW carries the full Practice-4 🟢/🟡/🔴
// classification + the 5-pattern dissolution ledger (modeling-
// discipline.md §4 — operator-ratified binding 2026-05-15; the doc
// enumerates FIVE patterns, the fifth being Parameterized-family).
// Records (`Conj` — `SpiceNetlist`, `ModelCard`, `Parameter`,
// `SpiceValue`, `SubcircuitDefinition`) are not coproducts and carry no
// ledger.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NamedNode — a non-ground node identity (PR #3168 codex review). A
// REUSABLE carrier for "this is a named node, not the global ground
// convention" — extracted from the `NodeRef.Named` variant so contexts
// that structurally exclude ground (`SubcircuitPorts`, future named-
// node-only carriers) can name `NamedNode` directly rather than rely
// on the absence of `Ground` in a permissive type like `Symbol`.
// Using `Symbol` as the port-element type achieved ground-exclusion
// only by being LESS permissive than `NodeRef` (Symbol can't carry the
// `Ground` variant) — a fact-by-absence rather than a positive type
// guarantee. `NamedNode` makes "named-node-not-ground" structural.
type NamedNode {
  name: Symbol
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NodeRef — a reference to a SPICE node. A node is a connection point
// between component terminals (Quarles 1993 §2). SPICE3 distinguishes
// the global ground convention (node `0`, the simulator's universal
// reference) from user-named nodes: ground is a SHARED simulator-level
// fact, not just a Symbol that happens to spell "0". Lifting that
// distinction into the type (PR #3168 codex review) makes "is this
// node ground?" a STRUCTURAL fact rather than a Symbol-spelling
// branch — preserving K-1's "kernel doesn't observe Symbol content"
// discipline at the data-model layer (no `if name == "0"` branches
// needed because there is no Symbol named "0" in well-formed data;
// ground is the `Ground` variant). The parser maps the literal "0" in
// the deck to `Ground`; the emitter maps `Ground` back to "0". `Symbol`
// content never leaves the parse/emit boundary.
//
// The `Named` variant wraps the standalone `NamedNode` carrier (PR
// #3168 codex review on commit c436bbfc) so contexts that structurally
// exclude ground can reuse `NamedNode` directly.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger —
// five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every netlist consumer (well-formedness
//      check, simulator-driver, emit) branches on ground-vs-named; a
//      shared classifier, not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: `Ground` is nullary (a singleton fact:
//      "this is the global reference"); `Named` wraps a `NamedNode`.
//      Collapsing to `{ identity: Symbol }` makes the ground convention
//      a spelling-sentinel ("0") that the kernel MUST read to recognize
//      ground — the opaque-strings-attract-heuristics anti-pattern
//      (node.dag K-1) and the M4 closed-sets-are-enums-not-strings
//      violation.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: ground-vs-named is the SPICE3 spec's own
//      partition of the node-name space, not a richer std/ algebra.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a node-ref is EXACTLY ONE of ground/named;
//      mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the variants are not `F<X>` over
//      a declared set; `Ground` is nullary and `Named` wraps a
//      `NamedNode` record — structurally heterogeneous, not a
//      mechanical copy.
// Terminal: the irreducible binary partition of the SPICE3 node-name
// space (global ground vs user-named).
type NodeRef
  = Ground
  | Named { node: NamedNode }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// SpiceValue — a lexical-surface value as it appears in a SPICE deck.
// SPICE values carry their own sub-grammar (decimal / scientific
// notation / suffix units `T`/`G`/`Meg`/`K`/`m`/`u`/`n`/`p`/`f` /
// parameter expressions). At the Shape-B netlist data-model layer this
// file owns the netlist's STATEMENT shape — the numeric sub-grammar is
// a separate format model (NOT in T-4.10's scope per "PROBE LIMITS"
// above). The lexeme is carried verbatim so `parse ∘ emit` is identity
// at the textual surface; numeric semantics is the simulator's concern.
// This is the same composition discipline json.dag uses for its
// arbitrary-precision number opt-in (RFC 8259 §6) — the format model
// names the carrier, the numeric model interprets it.
type SpiceValue {
  lexeme: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Parameter — a `name=value` pair (e.g., `L=2u`, `W=0.5u`, `AREA=2`).
// Used by the parameterized component variants that carry a
// `parameters: List<Parameter>` slot in this cut (`Mosfet`, `Bjt`,
// `SubcircuitCall`), by `.MODEL` parameter sets, and by `.OPTIONS`
// directives (Quarles 1993 §11.3, §15). The remaining variants without
// a `parameters` slot (`Resistor`, `Capacitor`, `Inductor`, `Diode`,
// `VoltageSource`, `CurrentSource`) are part of the same follow-up
// that enumerates the missing-list (E, F, G, H, J, O, S, T, U, W, Z)
// — see `Component`'s 🟡 trigger; the
// `Parameter` carrier is ready for them. `name` is a `Symbol`
// (K-1-opaque); `value` is a `SpiceValue` (lexeme; sub-grammar deferred
// per `SpiceValue`).
//
// PARAMETER-NAME SCHEMA AS PROJECTION (Practice 7). Which names are
// valid for which element kind is a CONSUMER-SIDE check, not a parallel
// field here — schema is a projection over the closed (Component-kind,
// SPICE3 §11) axis. So `parameters: List<Parameter>` carries the
// LEXICAL-SURFACE instance modifiers losslessly; an invalid name (e.g.
// `Bjt { parameters: [Parameter{name=NONSENSE, ...}] }`) is rejected
// by the spec-projection check, not by the type system. A typed sub-
// coproduct per element kind enumerating its valid parameter names is
// the eventual 🟢 destination (tied to `Component`'s 🟡 trigger);
// `List<Parameter>` is the intermediate scaffold consistent across
// every variant that carries instance modifiers.
//
// FLAG-FORM INSTANCE MODIFIERS (`OFF`, etc.) — SPICE3 §11 spells some
// instance modifiers as BARE FLAGS (`Q1 c b e m OFF`) rather than
// `name=value`. The intermediate scaffold encodes a flag as
// `Parameter { name = OFF (Symbol), value = SpiceValue { lexeme = "" } }`
// — an admissible workaround at the lexeme layer, surfaced honestly
// here. A typed `Modifier = Flag(Symbol) | KeyValue(Symbol, SpiceValue)`
// sub-coproduct is the same dissolution destination as the parameter-
// name schema, deferred to the same trigger.
type Parameter {
  name: Symbol
  value: SpiceValue
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// SubstrateSpec — the optional 4th terminal of a SPICE3 Q-card. The
// SPICE3 BJT statement permits both
//   `Qname COLLECTOR BASE EMITTER MODEL [params...]`             (3-term)
//   `Qname COLLECTOR BASE EMITTER SUBSTRATE MODEL [params...]`   (4-term)
// (Quarles 1993 §11.4). The two are distinct at the SYNTACTIC surface
// — `parse ∘ emit` must preserve the choice — even though they map to
// the same circuit topology when SUBSTRATE = node 0 (the simulator's
// default ground substrate, semantically). Modeling this as
// `Option<NodeRef>` is explicitly rejected by v4 discipline
// (std/diagnostic.dag header — `Correction = Suggested(Node) |
// Unavailable(NoCorrectionReason)`, NOT `Option<Node>`); the closed
// two-variant typed sum is the P2-faithful shape.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger —
// five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every Bjt-consumer (parse round-trip,
//      emit, simulator-driver) dispatches on the substrate-explicit-vs-
//      implicit fact; shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: `Implicit` is nullary; `Explicit`
//      carries a `NodeRef`. Collapsing to `{ explicit: Bool, node?:
//      NodeRef }` makes "explicit with no node" + "implicit with a
//      node" representable — the inverse of P2.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: the substrate-presence choice is a
//      SPICE3 syntactic-surface fact, not an algebra carrier.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a Q-card either DOES or DOES NOT spell the
//      substrate node; mutually-exclusive alternatives, never
//      simultaneous coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the two variants are not `F<X>`
//      over a declared set; `Implicit` is nullary and `Explicit`
//      carries a `NodeRef` — structurally distinct, not a mechanical
//      copy of any source set.
// Terminal: the irreducible binary syntactic-surface choice of the
// SPICE3 BJT substrate slot.
type SubstrateSpec
  = SubstrateImplicit
  | SubstrateExplicit { node: NodeRef }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Component — one element instance in a SPICE netlist. Coproduct over a
// supported subset of the SPICE3 element kinds (Quarles 1993 §11). Each
// variant carries NAMED terminal slots fixing its arity by construction
// (P2 illegal states unrepresentable: a `Resistor` with three terminals
// is not a representable value of this type). See modeling notes
// "WHY CLOSED `Component` COPRODUCT" for the design rationale.
//
// HONESTY NOTE (PR #3168 codex review). The 10 variants below are a
// SUBSET of SPICE3 elements (FIDELITY SCOPE deliverable (2) gap;
// header top), AND each enumerated variant carries only
// the MANDATORY-positional facts for its element — not the full per-
// element statement grammar (the optional parameters/source-spec
// transient forms). These are two distinct coverage gaps tracked in
// the dissolution trigger below:
//   (a) ELEMENT-KIND COVERAGE — enumerated: R, C, L (passives);
//       V, I (independent sources); D, Q, M (semiconductor devices);
//       K (mutual inductance); X (subcircuit invocation). NOT yet
//       enumerated: E, F, G, H (linear controlled sources), J (JFET),
//       O (LTRA lossy transmission line), S (voltage-controlled
//       switch), T (lossless transmission line), U (URC uniform
//       distributed RC line), W (current-controlled switch), Z
//       (MESFET). Quarles 1993 SPICE3F5 §11. This list MUST stay
//       synchronized with the SPICE3F5 anchor's element table — if a
//       SPICE3 element is neither in `type Component` above nor in
//       this missing-list, the 🟡 trigger could false-fire green while
//       leaving a valid spec element unmodeled (the M3 / P1 fidelity
//       failure mode codex review #3168 surfaced for the prior
//       omission of Z, O, U).
//   (b) PER-VARIANT GRAMMAR COVERAGE — each enumerated variant is a
//       MANDATORY-FIELDS-ONLY shape; the spec's optional facts are
//       not yet modeled:
//         - Resistor / Capacitor / Inductor: optional `TC=...`
//           temperature coefficients, `TEMP=...`, IC / initial-current
//           for L; optional `.MODEL` reference (R/C/L can be model-
//           referenced in SPICE3 §11.1).
//         - Diode: optional `AREA`, `OFF`, `IC=Vd`.
//         - Bjt: substrate-spec (optional 4th terminal) and instance
//           parameters (`AREA`/`OFF`/`IC=Vbe,Vce`) ADDRESSED — `Bjt`
//           now carries `substrate: SubstrateSpec` (typed sum; see the
//           `SubstrateSpec` declaration above for the 5-pattern ledger)
//           and `parameters: List<Parameter>` (matching `Mosfet`'s
//           instance-modifier slot; flag-form `OFF` encoded as an
//           empty-lexeme `Parameter`, see `Parameter`'s "FLAG-FORM"
//           note). The `parameters` carrier is the lexical-surface
//           scaffold; the typed parameter-name schema is the same
//           per-element dissolution work as for `Mosfet`.
//         - Mosfet: optional `L`/`W` (already in `parameters`),
//           `AD`/`AS`/`PD`/`PS`/`NRD`/`NRS`/`OFF`/`IC=Vds,Vgs,Vbs` —
//           current `parameters: List<Parameter>` admits these by
//           lexeme but does not type-enforce their names.
//         - VoltageSource / CurrentSource: SPICE3 §11.2 transient
//           source spec — `DC`/`AC` magnitude+phase + `PULSE`/`SIN`/
//           `EXP`/`PWL`/`SFFM` parameterized waveforms. The current
//           `value: SpiceValue` slot carries only the DC magnitude
//           lexeme.
//         - MutualInductance: complete for the basic form (linear
//           mutual inductance, no `.MODEL` reference); SPICE3 also
//           supports nonlinear-core model-referenced form.
//         - SubcircuitCall: complete by construction (the
//           `connections: SubcircuitConnections` non-empty carrier +
//           `parameters` list span the X-element syntax;
//           `connections` mirrors `SubcircuitDefinition.ports`'s
//           non-empty discipline at the call-site boundary per
//           PR #3168 codex review).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟡 YELLOW (scaffold). The SUM's
// structural shape (one variant per SPICE3 element kind with named
// terminal slots) is the right form; what makes it YELLOW is the two
// coverage gaps enumerated above — the richer source (the full
// SPICE3 §11 element grammar) exists in the spec but is not yet
// modeled here.
//   Named trigger: when (a) all SPICE3F5 elements named in the
//   missing-list above (E, F, G, H, J, O, S, T, U, W, Z) are added
//   as variants AND (b) each enumerated variant carries its
//   full SPICE3 §11 statement grammar (optional parameters made
//   structural rather than lexeme-only; transient source specs as
//   typed sub-coproducts; optional terminals via per-variant named
//   slots — NOT by `Option<NodeRef>` or sentinel value), the SUM
//   reclassifies 🟢 GREEN. Tied to `ModelKind`'s 🟡 trigger below
//   (the two dissolve as one sweep — same SPICE3 authority).
// Five patterns attempted on the SUM (all FAIL to dissolve it):
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every netlist consumer (well-formedness
//      check, simulator-driver, emit) dispatches on ALL kinds (a
//      netlist walker handles each element type uniformly); kind is a
//      shared classifier, not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the variants carry STRUCTURALLY
//      heterogeneous payloads — Resistor has `n_plus`/`n_minus`/`value`
//      (2 terminals + a value); Mosfet has `drain`/`gate`/`source`/
//      `body`/`model`/`parameters` (4 terminals + a model ref + a
//      parameter list); MutualInductance has `l1`/`l2` (Symbol refs to
//      other inductors) + a `coupling` value, not terminals at all.
//      Collapsing to `{ kind, terminals?, value?, model?, params? }`
//      makes illegal-shape combinations (a Mosfet with no `drain`, a
//      Resistor carrying a model ref) representable — the inverse of
//      P2.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: a SPICE element is not an algebra carrier;
//      no richer std/ algebraic source the variants trace to. The
//      partition is the SPICE3 spec's own enumeration, not a
//      composition of algebraic facts.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a component is EXACTLY ONE element kind
//      (an R is not also a C); mutually-exclusive alternatives, never
//      simultaneous coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the variants are NOT `F<X>` over a
//      declared set — their payloads are structurally distinct (2- vs
//      3- vs 4-terminal slots, value-vs-model-vs-nary-parameters), not
//      a mechanical copy of an enumerable source set. A `Component
//      { kind: Symbol, terminals: List<NodeRef>, params: List<Parameter>
//      }` projection over an `ElementSpec` table was attempted (see
//      modeling notes design (a)) and rejected because it makes
//      illegal arities representable — Practice-7 fails on the same
//      structural-heterogeneity ground that Practice-2 (variant-is-
//      data) does.
// On dissolution (when the trigger fires) the SUM IS the closed SPICE3
// element set as a coproduct with per-kind terminal-arity AND per-kind
// statement-grammar enforced structurally; cost-of-change is 1 (per-
// variant edit), no parallel representation forced elsewhere.
type Component
  = Resistor { identity: Symbol, n_plus: NodeRef, n_minus: NodeRef, value: SpiceValue }
  | Capacitor { identity: Symbol, n_plus: NodeRef, n_minus: NodeRef, value: SpiceValue }
  | Inductor { identity: Symbol, n_plus: NodeRef, n_minus: NodeRef, value: SpiceValue }
  | VoltageSource { identity: Symbol, n_plus: NodeRef, n_minus: NodeRef, value: SpiceValue }
  | CurrentSource { identity: Symbol, n_plus: NodeRef, n_minus: NodeRef, value: SpiceValue }
  | Diode { identity: Symbol, anode: NodeRef, cathode: NodeRef, model: Symbol }
  | Bjt { identity: Symbol, collector: NodeRef, base: NodeRef, emitter: NodeRef, substrate: SubstrateSpec, model: Symbol, parameters: List<Parameter> }
  | Mosfet { identity: Symbol, drain: NodeRef, gate: NodeRef, source: NodeRef, body: NodeRef, model: Symbol, parameters: List<Parameter> }
  | MutualInductance { identity: Symbol, l1: Symbol, l2: Symbol, coupling: SpiceValue }
  | SubcircuitCall { identity: Symbol, connections: SubcircuitConnections, subcircuit: Symbol, parameters: List<Parameter> }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ModelKind — the supported subset of `.MODEL` device kinds in SPICE3.
// Each `.MODEL` card declares a NAMED parameter set for one of these
// device types (Quarles 1993 §11.3 + §6); component instances reference
// the model by name (`Diode.model`, `Bjt.model`, `Mosfet.model`).
//
// HONESTY NOTE (PR #3168 codex review): this enum is a SUBSET of the
// SPICE3 `.MODEL` authority — the FIDELITY SCOPE deliverable (2)
// gap (header top), not the SHAPE deliverable (1). The full SPICE3 set is R (resistor model),
// C (capacitor model), URC (uniform RC line), LTRA (lossy transmission
// line), SW (voltage-controlled switch), CSW (current-controlled
// switch), D (diode), NPN, PNP, NJF (N-JFET), PJF (P-JFET), NMOS, PMOS,
// NMF (N-MESFET), PMF (P-MESFET) — Quarles 1993 §6. The five variants
// below match the five `Component` variants that carry a `model: Symbol`
// slot in this initial cut (`Diode` / `Bjt`(N|P) / `Mosfet`(N|P)); the
// classification reflects that incomplete coverage rather than papering
// over it.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟡 YELLOW (scaffold). The SUM's
// structural shape (a closed enumeration of SPICE3 device kinds) is
// terminal; what makes it YELLOW is its INCOMPLETE COVERAGE of the
// closed SPICE3 authority — the richer source (the remaining ten
// .MODEL kinds enumerated above) exists in the spec but is not yet
// modeled here.
//   Named trigger: when (a) `Component`'s 🟡 trigger fires (the full
//   SPICE3 element set is enumerated — see Component above), AND
//   (b) the model-referencing variants among them (every Component
//   variant that carries a `model: Symbol` slot) have their
//   corresponding `.MODEL` kind enumerated here — `ModelKind`
//   reclassifies 🟢 GREEN. The two coverage gaps dissolve as one
//   sweep because they share the SAME authority (the SPICE3 spec) and
//   the same per-element work (a Component variant that references a
//   model has a matching ModelKind, by construction of the spec).
// Five patterns attempted on the SUM (all FAIL to dissolve it):
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every `.MODEL`-consumer (parameter-
//      schema checker, emitter) dispatches on the model kind to know
//      which parameter names are valid; shared classifier, not
//      scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing the closed kind set to a
//      `{ name: Symbol }` field makes the kind opaque — the parameter-
//      schema check loses its dispatch target and degenerates to a
//      string match on the spelling (the M4 closed-sets-are-enums-not-
//      strings violation; feedback_opaque_strings_attract_heuristics).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: a device-model kind is not an algebra
//      carrier; it is the SPICE3 spec's enumeration of device classes,
//      not a composition of std/ algebraic facts.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a `.MODEL` card has EXACTLY ONE device
//      kind; alternatives, never coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the variants share no `F<X>`
//      shape — they are bare nullary discriminants (the per-kind
//      parameter schema lives at the `ModelCard.parameters` slot, NOT
//      as variant payload; the schema is itself a separately
//      enumerable concern the consuming check projects over). No
//      mechanical copy of a source set; not a Practice-7 projection.
type ModelKind
  = DiodeModel
  | BjtNpn
  | BjtPnp
  | MosfetNmos
  | MosfetPmos


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ModelCard — a `.MODEL name kind ( param=value ... )` declaration. A
// model card binds a NAME to a parameter set under a given device kind;
// component instances reference the name (Quarles 1993 §6).
//
// `parameters` is the kind-specific parameter set — the schema (which
// names are valid for which `ModelKind`) is a consumer-side check, not
// a parallel field here (Practice 7: schema is a projection over the
// kind set). This file does not enumerate per-kind schemas — that is a
// SPICE3-spec lookup the consumer performs.
type ModelCard {
  name: Symbol
  kind: ModelKind
  parameters: List<Parameter>
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// AcSweepKind — the sweep kind of an `.AC` analysis directive (Quarles
// 1993 §11.5.1): linear, decade, or octave sweep.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger —
// five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: the sweep kind is consumed uniformly by
//      the analysis-driver and the emitter; shared, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing to `{ name: Symbol }`
//      reduces the closed three-way kind to an opaque-string proxy
//      (M4 violation).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: a frequency-sweep kind is not an algebra
//      carrier; it is the SPICE3 spec's enumeration.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a sweep is EXACTLY ONE kind; mutually-
//      exclusive alternatives, never simultaneous coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the three are bare nullary
//      discriminants; no `F<X>` shape, no separately-declared source
//      set to project over.
// Terminal: the closed AC-analysis sweep-kind enumeration.
type AcSweepKind
  = Linear
  | Decade
  | Octave


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NodeVoltageBinding — one `V(node) = value` assignment inside a
// `.IC` / `.NODESET` card (Quarles 1993 §11.5.4). Both directives
// are VARIADIC at the SPICE3 card surface — a single `.IC` or
// `.NODESET` may carry an arbitrary number of bindings:
//   `.IC V(1)=5V V(out)=2.5V V(in)=0`
//   `.NODESET V(out)=2.5V V(n3)=1.2V`
// One card maps to one `Directive` value carrying `assignments:
// List<NodeVoltageBinding>` — see `InitialCondition` / `NodeSet`
// below. Modeling each binding as its own directive variant would
// SILENTLY NORMALIZE one card into multiple directive facts, breaking
// `parse ∘ emit` round-trip fidelity (the user wrote one card; emit
// must produce one card).
type NodeVoltageBinding {
  node: NodeRef
  value: SpiceValue
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NodeVoltageBindings — the non-empty list of bindings carried by one
// `.IC` or `.NODESET` card (PR #3168 codex review). SPICE3F5 §11.5.4
// requires at least one `V(node)=value` binding per card; a card with
// zero bindings is a syntactic dead-end (no information to inject).
//
// Modeled with the same head+tail discipline as `SubcircuitPorts` so
// non-emptiness is STRUCTURALLY ENFORCED at the type layer rather than
// as a well-formedness check — INVARIANTS P2 / modeling-discipline.md
// Practice 2/6 (illegal states unrepresentable). A zero-binding
// `NodeVoltageBindings` value is unconstructible because `first` is
// mandatory; the type system rejects empty input.
//
// Same eventual dissolution as `SubcircuitPorts`: a generic
// `NonEmptyList<T>` in std/ would absorb both head+tail records; the
// inlined record is the worker-tier scaffold that does not require
// that substrate addition.
type NodeVoltageBindings {
  first: NodeVoltageBinding
  rest: List<NodeVoltageBinding>
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Directive — a SPICE analysis or control statement (the dot-prefixed
// cards; Quarles 1993 §11.5). Each variant carries the parameters the
// spec requires for that directive.
//
// `.END` IS NOT A `Directive` VARIANT (PR #3168 codex review). `.END`
// is the SPICE3 FILE TERMINATOR (Quarles 1993 §2.4): exactly one,
// always last, never repeatable. Placing it as a `Directive` variant
// + carrying it in a `directives: List<Directive>` admits illegal
// states (zero `.End`s, multiple `.End`s, `.End` not in tail
// position) — INVARIANTS P2 violation, the same shape modeling-
// discipline.md Practice 2/6 forbid in `Component`. The file
// terminator is owned by the parse/emit DISCIPLINE, not the data
// model: parse success structurally requires exactly one trailing
// `.END` (absence / duplication / mid-position is a Diagnostic);
// emit always appends `.END` after the last directive. There is no
// data shape it could correctly take here.
//
// HONESTY NOTE (PR #3168 codex review). The Directive coverage has
// TWO distinct gaps tracked by the YELLOW trigger below, parallel to
// `Component`'s structure — both gaps fall under the FIDELITY SCOPE
// deliverable (2) (header top), not the SHAPE deliverable (1):
//   (a) DIRECTIVE-KIND COVERAGE — enumerated: `.OP`, `.TRAN`, `.DC`,
//       `.AC`, `.IC`, `.NODESET`, `.OPTIONS`, `.INCLUDE`. NOT yet
//       enumerated: `.NOISE`, `.TF`, `.SENS`, `.DISTO`, `.FOUR`,
//       `.PZ` (pole-zero), `.PRINT`, `.PLOT`, `.SAVE`, `.WIDTH`,
//       `.LIB`. Quarles 1993 SPICE3F5 §11.5. This list MUST stay
//       synchronized with the SPICE3F5 anchor's analysis/control card
//       table — same synchronization discipline as `Component` above;
//       a missing directive name lets the 🟡 trigger false-fire green
//       (M3 / P1 failure mode codex review #3168 surfaced for the
//       prior omission of `.PZ`).
//   (b) PER-VARIANT GRAMMAR COVERAGE — each enumerated variant is a
//       MANDATORY-FIELDS-ONLY shape; the spec's optional facts are
//       not yet modeled:
//         - Tran: optional `TSTART` (default 0), `TMAX` (default
//           (TSTOP-TSTART)/50), and flag `UIC` (use initial
//           conditions) — Quarles 1993 §11.5.7. Current shape is
//           `{ tstep, tstop }` only.
//         - Dc: optional second-source nested sweep
//           `[SRC2 VSTART2 VSTOP2 VINCR2]` — Quarles 1993 §11.5.2.
//           Current shape is single-source only.
//         - InitialCondition / NodeSet: variadic per SPICE3 §11.5.4
//           (`.IC V(n)=v V(n)=v ...`); ADDRESSED — both variants
//           carry `assignments: List<NodeVoltageBinding>` (see
//           `NodeVoltageBinding` declaration above). One `.IC` card
//           maps to one `InitialCondition` value with N bindings;
//           `parse ∘ emit` round-trip preserves single-card identity
//           (no silent normalization into N directives). Fix landed
//           per PR #3168 openai-pro review.
//         - Ac / Op / Options / Include: complete-for-mandatory-grammar
//           at this layer. `.AC` is `.AC SWEEP N FSTART FSTOP`
//           (4 mandatory fields, no optional); `.OP` is nullary;
//           `.OPTIONS` is variadic via `parameters: List<Parameter>`;
//           `.INCLUDE` carries one filename. Surfaced honestly so any
//           spec re-read can flag a miss.
//
// `.End`'s absence from this SUM is NOT part of the YELLOW debt — by
// construction it does not belong here at all (file-terminator note
// above).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟡 YELLOW (scaffold). The SUM's
// structural shape (one variant per SPICE3 analysis/control directive
// with named mandatory-parameter slots) is the right form; what makes
// it YELLOW is the two coverage gaps above.
//   Named trigger: when (a) all SPICE3F5 §11.5 directives from the
//   missing-list are added as variants (each with its mandatory-
//   parameter slots typed structurally, no `parameters: List<
//   Parameter>` catch-all) AND (b) each enumerated variant carries
//   its full SPICE3F5 §11.5 grammar (Tran's TSTART/TMAX/UIC; Dc's
//   optional 2nd-source sweep, modeled either as an Implicit/Explicit
//   typed sum like `SubstrateSpec` or as a nested-sweep variant), the
//   SUM reclassifies 🟢 GREEN. Tied conceptually to `Component`'s 🟡
//   trigger (same SPICE3 authority + same per-variant-grammar
//   discipline), but a separate sweep because the directive set is
//   disjoint from the element set.
// Five patterns attempted on the SUM (all FAIL to dissolve it):
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every netlist consumer (analysis-driver,
//      emitter) dispatches on the directive kind to know what to do
//      (run a transient analysis, set an initial condition, include a
//      file); shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the variants carry STRUCTURALLY
//      heterogeneous payloads — `Tran` carries `tstep` + `tstop`;
//      `Dc` carries a swept source + range; `Ac` carries a sweep kind
//      + `n` + frequency range; `Op` is nullary; `Options` carries a
//      parameter list. Collapsing to `{ kind, parameters: List<...> }`
//      makes illegal-shape combinations (a `Tran` with no `tstep`)
//      representable — the inverse of P2.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: a SPICE directive is not an algebra
//      carrier; it is the spec's enumeration of analysis + control
//      commands.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a directive is EXACTLY ONE kind; mutually-
//      exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the variants are structurally
//      heterogeneous (different field counts and shapes), not `F<X>`
//      over a declared set; not a Practice-7 projection.
// On dissolution (when the trigger fires) the SUM IS the closed
// SPICE3 directive set as a coproduct with per-kind payload shape
// enforced structurally; cost-of-change is 1 (per-variant edit), no
// parallel representation forced elsewhere.
type Directive
  = Op
  | Tran { tstep: SpiceValue, tstop: SpiceValue }
  | Dc { source: Symbol, vstart: SpiceValue, vstop: SpiceValue, vincr: SpiceValue }
  | Ac { sweep: AcSweepKind, n: SpiceValue, fstart: SpiceValue, fstop: SpiceValue }
  | InitialCondition { assignments: NodeVoltageBindings }
  | NodeSet { assignments: NodeVoltageBindings }
  | Options { parameters: List<Parameter> }
  | Include { path: String }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// SubcircuitStatement — what is legal INSIDE a `.SUBCKT/.ENDS` body
// (PR #3168 codex review). SPICE3F5 §3.4 permits exactly three
// statement kinds inside a subcircuit body:
//   - Component instances (R/C/L/V/I/D/Q/M/K/X/...) — the topology.
//   - `.MODEL` cards local to the subcircuit.
//   - `.INCLUDE` directives — the ONLY directive legal inside a
//     subcircuit; `.INCLUDE` is preprocessing-style (substitute the
//     named file's contents at this position), not an analysis/
//     control card. The earlier "all directives forbidden" framing was
//     too coarse; it forbade `.INCLUDE` along with the genuinely-
//     illegal analysis/control cards.
// Nested `.SUBCKT/.ENDS` pairs are LIFTED to the top-level list with
// `parent_scope` carrying lexical identity (modeling notes "SUBCIRCUIT
// NESTING + LEXICAL SCOPING"); after lifting, a nested subcircuit
// definition is NOT a `SubcircuitStatement` — it is its own top-level
// `SubcircuitDefinition` value with a non-empty `parent_scope`.
//
// SubcircuitStatement is an ordered statement; a `body: List<
// SubcircuitStatement>` preserves the original deck's interleaving of
// component instances / `.MODEL` cards / `.INCLUDE` directives,
// supporting `parse ∘ emit` round-trip identity at the textual surface.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger —
// five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every subcircuit-body consumer (well-
//      formedness, emit, simulator-driver) dispatches on the statement
//      kind to know what to do; shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: payloads are structurally distinct
//      (`StmtComponent` carries a `Component`; `StmtModel` carries a
//      `ModelCard`; `StmtInclude` carries a `String` path). Collapsing
//      to `{ kind, component?, model?, path? }` makes illegal-shape
//      combinations representable (the inverse of P2).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: the SPICE3 subcircuit-body legality is
//      a spec partition, not a richer std/ algebra.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a subcircuit statement is EXACTLY ONE of
//      Component / ModelCard / Include — mutually-exclusive
//      alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: payloads are structurally
//      distinct (one wraps `Component`, one wraps `ModelCard`, one
//      wraps `String`) — not `F<X>` over a separately-declared set.
// Terminal: the closed three-way partition of legal-in-`.SUBCKT`
// statements per SPICE3F5 §3.4. The set is closed (forbidden cards
// are explicit in the spec); adding a fourth requires either a SPICE3
// spec change (operator-tier) or evidence of a missed legal-in-SUBCKT
// statement (would be a worker-tier addition with named justification).
type SubcircuitStatement
  = StmtComponent { component: Component }
  | StmtModel { model: ModelCard }
  | StmtInclude { path: String }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// SubcircuitDefinition — a `.SUBCKT name (terminals) ... .ENDS`
// declaration (Quarles 1993 §3.4). A subcircuit defines a NAMED
// parameterized topology that `Component.SubcircuitCall` instances
// invoke by name. The body is a single ordered list of
// `SubcircuitStatement`s (the three legal-in-`.SUBCKT` statement kinds
// per SPICE3F5 §3.4 — see `SubcircuitStatement` above for the closed
// partition and the legality reasoning).
//
// PORTS EXCLUDE GROUND AND ARE NON-EMPTY (PR #3168 codex + openai-pro
// reviews). The subcircuit port carrier (`ports: SubcircuitPorts` —
// see the `SubcircuitPorts` declaration below) enforces TWO SPICE3
// constraints structurally:
//   (a) NO GROUND — the carrier holds `NamedNode`s (the standalone
//       non-ground node-identity carrier declared with `NodeRef`),
//       not `NodeRef`s, so the global ground convention
//       (`NodeRef.Ground`) is NOT a legal port. Using `NamedNode` as
//       the element type makes "port-is-named-not-ground" a positive
//       type fact (a `NamedNode` value is, by construction, not the
//       ground variant), not an absence-by-permissiveness. Port names
//       are LOCAL-scope identifiers the call-site connects to its own
//       node-refs; ground is the simulator's universal reference and
//       cannot be a formal parameter.
//   (b) NON-EMPTY — `SubcircuitPorts` is a head+tail record
//       (`{ first: NamedNode, rest: List<NamedNode> }`) so a zero-port
//       subcircuit is STRUCTURALLY UNREPRESENTABLE. SPICE3F5 §2.4
//       requires `.SUBCKT` external nodes to be present; a zero-port
//       `.SUBCKT name` is a syntactic dead-end the type system now
//       rejects rather than admits as a well-formedness check.
// Both constraints are type-enforced, not check-enforced — INVARIANTS
// P2 / modeling-discipline.md Practice 2/6 (illegal states
// unrepresentable). Inside the subcircuit BODY, component terminals
// are still `NodeRef` and CAN reference ground — `V(0)` is the
// simulator's universal reference, accessible from any scope.
//
// PRIOR "NO `directives` FIELD" RATIONALE (PR #3168 earlier codex
// review). The prior shape carried `directives: List<Directive>` here
// and tracked the in-SUBCKT-prohibition as a "consumer-side well-
// formedness check" — but INVARIANTS P2 / modeling-discipline.md
// Practice 2/6 require illegal states UNREPRESENTABLE, not merely
// checked. The current `body: List<SubcircuitStatement>` keeps that
// type-enforcement (only the three legal-in-SUBCKT statement kinds
// are representable; an `.OP` inside `.SUBCKT` is structurally
// unrepresentable) AND correctly admits the legal-in-SUBCKT statement
// `.INCLUDE` that the earlier "no directives" framing had over-
// excluded. The prohibition remains type-enforced; the legal-set is
// now SPICE3-faithful.
//
// NESTED `.SUBCKT` LEXICAL SCOPING (PR #3168 codex review). SPICE3F5
// §3.4 admits nested `.SUBCKT` definitions where each name is LEXICALLY
// SCOPED to its enclosing subcircuit: a `.SUBCKT FOO` defined inside
// `.SUBCKT BAR` is a different definition from a `.SUBCKT FOO` defined
// inside `.SUBCKT QUUX` or at the top level. The prior shape lifted
// nested subcircuits to a flat top-level list with bare `Symbol`-keyed
// resolution — which ERASED that scoping and made same-named local
// definitions in different parents collide as competing authorities
// (P1 modeling-faithfulness / P2 ambiguous representable states).
// The current shape lifts to a flat list BUT preserves the lexical
// scope structurally via `parent_scope: List<Symbol>` on each
// `SubcircuitDefinition`:
//   - `parent_scope = []` ⇒ defined at top-level.
//   - `parent_scope = [BAR]` ⇒ defined inside `.SUBCKT BAR`.
//   - `parent_scope = [BAZ, BAR]` ⇒ defined inside `.SUBCKT BAR`
//     inside `.SUBCKT BAZ` (outermost-first, list-depth = nesting-
//     depth).
// Two `.SUBCKT FOO`s in different parents now have DISTINCT
// `(name, parent_scope)` identities at the type layer — they are no
// longer competing authorities. `SubcircuitCall.subcircuit` resolution
// is positional: the call's containing list (the top-level
// `SpiceNetlist.body` list wrapping it as `DeckComponent`, or its enclosing
// `SubcircuitDefinition.body` list wrapping it as `StmtComponent`)
// determines the call's enclosing scope; lookup walks up the scope
// chain per
// SPICE3 §3.4 shadowing rules (innermost first, then outward, then
// top-level). This is consumer-side logic over the data model; the
// data model preserves the facts the consumer needs.
// This avoids introducing a recursive `SubcircuitDefinition` type
// (which would parallel node.dag's A1 sole-recursive-type discipline
// for a non-substrate domain carrier, and is a substrate-design call
// that exceeds worker authority); `List<Symbol>` is the kernel-ambient
// flat carrier that captures the scoping faithfully without that
// commitment.
type SubcircuitDefinition {
  name: Symbol
  parent_scope: List<Symbol>
  ports: SubcircuitPorts
  body: List<SubcircuitStatement>
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// SubcircuitPorts — the non-empty list of formal port names declared
// after `.SUBCKT name` (PR #3168 openai-pro review). SPICE3F5 §2.4
// requires a subcircuit to declare at least one external node; a
// `.SUBCKT name` with zero ports is a syntactic dead-end (no way to
// connect the subcircuit from a call site).
//
// Modeled as a head+tail record so non-emptiness is STRUCTURALLY
// ENFORCED at the type layer rather than as a well-formedness check —
// INVARIANTS P2 / modeling-discipline.md Practice 2/6 (illegal states
// unrepresentable). A zero-port `SubcircuitPorts` value is
// unconstructible because `first` is mandatory; the type system
// rejects empty input.
//
// Why a record instead of a typed sum (`OnePort | MultiPort`): the
// head+tail record is the canonical std/ non-empty-list shape and
// adds no enumeration overhead per arity bucket; consumers iterate
// the conceptual sequence as `cons(first, rest)`. A non-empty-list
// REFINEMENT type in std/ would be the eventual substrate dissolution
// (a generic `NonEmptyList<T>` carrier), but that is std/-substrate-
// tier and not in T-4.10's scope; the inlined head+tail record is the
// honest scaffold and uses only the kernel-ambient `List` carrier.
//
// PORT-ELEMENT TYPE: `NamedNode` (PR #3168 codex review on commit
// c436bbfc). The prior shape used `Symbol` as the element type, which
// achieved ground-exclusion only by being LESS PERMISSIVE than
// `NodeRef` (Symbol can't carry the `Ground` variant) — a guarantee
// by absence rather than a positive type fact. Using the standalone
// `NamedNode` carrier (declared above with `NodeRef`) makes
// "subcircuit-port-element is a named node, not ground" a STRUCTURAL
// type fact: a value of `NamedNode` is, by construction, not the
// ground variant of `NodeRef`. Same Symbol payload at the bottom; the
// difference is whether the type-level signal carries the engineering
// invariant or relies on it being unreachable in a permissive parent
// type.
type SubcircuitPorts {
  first: NamedNode
  rest: List<NamedNode>
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// SubcircuitConnections — the non-empty list of node-refs an
// X-element (subcircuit call) connects to its target subcircuit's
// formal ports (PR #3168 codex review). The call-site mirror of
// `SubcircuitPorts`: SPICE3F5 §3.4's `X NAME N1 N2 ... SUBCKTNAME
// [PARAMS]` form requires at least one connection node (matching the
// callee subcircuit's non-empty port arity), and a well-formed call
// has the SAME arity as the callee's `ports`. The arity-match
// invariant is a consumer-side check (it requires resolving
// `subcircuit: Symbol` against the SubcircuitDefinition table to
// know the callee's port count); the non-emptiness invariant is
// type-enforced HERE.
//
// Element type is `NodeRef` (not `NamedNode`) because the call site
// CAN connect a callee port to global ground (the simulator's
// universal reference) — e.g. `X1 in 0 out my_amp` is legal: the
// middle port is connected to ground at the call site. (Callee
// ports themselves cannot BE ground; that's `SubcircuitPorts`'s
// `NamedNode` discipline at the SubcircuitDefinition side.)
//
// Same head+tail discipline + same eventual std/-substrate
// dissolution as `SubcircuitPorts` and `NodeVoltageBindings`: a
// generic `NonEmptyList<T>` carrier would absorb all three. The
// inlined record is the worker-tier scaffold that does not require
// that substrate addition.
type SubcircuitConnections {
  first: NodeRef
  rest: List<NodeRef>
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// DeckStatement — one statement in the top-level deck body
// (PR #3168 codex review). SPICE3 admits four statement kinds at the
// top level — component instances, `.MODEL` cards, `.SUBCKT/.ENDS`
// definitions, and analysis/control directives (including `.INCLUDE`)
// — and a real deck INTERLEAVES them in source order (e.g. `.MODEL`
// card → R1 element → `.INCLUDE foo.inc` → Q2 element). The prior
// `SpiceNetlist` shape carried four PARALLEL lists (`components`,
// `models`, `subcircuits`, `directives`) which silently DROPPED the
// interleave order — a deck whose source order was `M-R-I-Q` would
// re-emit as `R-Q | M | I` (some canonical bucket-ordered form),
// breaking `parse ∘ emit` textual round-trip (P1 modeling-faithfulness
// / P3 facts-flow-forward — the source-order fact existed at parse,
// then dropped). The file already preserves ordered statements at the
// subcircuit-body layer via `SubcircuitStatement`; this coproduct
// applies the same discipline at the top level.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger —
// five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every deck consumer (well-formedness
//      check, emit, simulator-driver) dispatches on the statement
//      kind; shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: payloads are structurally distinct
//      (`DeckComponent` carries a `Component`; `DeckModelCard`
//      carries a `ModelCard`; `DeckSubcircuit` carries a
//      `SubcircuitDefinition`; `DeckDirective` carries a
//      `Directive`). Collapsing to a flat record makes illegal-shape
//      combinations representable (the inverse of P2).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: the SPICE3 top-level statement set is a
//      spec partition, not a richer std/ algebra.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a deck statement is EXACTLY ONE of the
//      four — mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: payloads are structurally
//      distinct (one wraps `Component`, one wraps `ModelCard`, one
//      wraps `SubcircuitDefinition`, one wraps `Directive`) — not
//      `F<X>` over a separately-declared set.
// Terminal: the closed four-way partition of SPICE3 top-level
// statement kinds. A fifth kind would be a SPICE3 spec change
// (operator-tier) or evidence of a missed legal-at-top-level
// statement (worker-tier addition with named justification).
type DeckStatement
  = DeckComponent { component: Component }
  | DeckModelCard { model: ModelCard }
  | DeckSubcircuit { definition: SubcircuitDefinition }
  | DeckDirective { directive: Directive }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// SpiceNetlist — the top-level deck. The title is the first line of
// any SPICE deck (Quarles 1993 §2); the spec treats it as a required
// label, not a comment.
//
// `.END` IS NOT A FIELD HERE (PR #3168 codex review). The SPICE3
// file terminator is a STRUCTURAL fact owned by parse/emit, not
// netlist data: parse success structurally requires exactly one
// trailing `.END` (absence / duplication / mid-position = Diagnostic);
// emit always appends `.END` after the last statement. Carrying it as
// a `Directive` variant in `body` or as a `terminator: End` field
// would admit zero / multiple / misplaced terminators as representable
// values — INVARIANTS P2. See the `Directive` declaration's header
// note for the full rationale.
//
// SINGLE ORDERED BODY (PR #3168 codex review). The body is a single
// `body: List<DeckStatement>` rather than four parallel lists for the
// four statement kinds — this preserves the deck's source-order
// interleaving of components / `.MODEL` cards / `.SUBCKT` definitions
// / directives, matching the `SubcircuitStatement` discipline at the
// subcircuit-body layer. A deck whose source order interleaves these
// kinds round-trips through `parse ∘ emit` faithfully; the prior
// parallel-list shape silently bucketed the statements and dropped
// the order.
//
// LIFTED NESTED `.SUBCKT` POSITION — 🟡 YELLOW scaffold (PR #3168
// cursor exploratory: tightening the named-trigger to mirror
// `Component` / `Directive` YELLOW shape). Nested `.SUBCKT/.ENDS`
// pairs are lifted to the top-level body (see "SUBCIRCUIT NESTING +
// LEXICAL SCOPING" in the top-of-file modeling notes). A lifted
// nested subcircuit appears in `body` as a `DeckSubcircuit` value
// with non-empty `parent_scope`; the parser places it at a canonical
// post-lifting position, and emit re-nests it inside the named parent
// `.SUBCKT/.ENDS` pair using the `parent_scope` chain. The exact
// within-parent position of a nested SUBCKT (i.e. which statement of
// the parent's body it appeared BETWEEN) is NOT preserved by this
// shape — only its parent identity is. This is a documented secondary
// fidelity concession (top-level interleave preserved; within-parent
// nested-SUBCKT position not preserved), favoring top-level
// interleave fidelity (the reviewer's flagged concern) over within-
// parent nesting position.
//   Named trigger: when a consumer requires byte-exact `parse ∘ emit`
//   round-trip of decks where a nested `.SUBCKT` is interleaved
//   between sibling statements of its parent body (rather than
//   appearing at the parent's start or end), this concession
//   dissolves. The fix paths are: (a) extend
//   `SubcircuitDefinition` with `position_in_parent: List<Symbol>`-
//   indexed positional metadata (a parallel ordering authority);
//   (b) admit `SubcircuitDefinition` as a `SubcircuitStatement`
//   variant + relax the lifting discipline (introduces a recursive
//   carrier, std/-substrate-tier per A1); (c) accept the
//   concession permanently and emit a lossless-but-not-byte-
//   identical canonicalization. The current shape blocks none of
//   these; the trigger is the first consumer asking for byte-exact
//   nested-SUBCKT round-trip.
//
// Emptiness of `body` is not structurally ruled out — SPICE3 admits a
// deck with only a title (semantically empty but syntactically legal).
// Consumer-side checks (simulator-driver) enforce semantic non-
// emptiness where applicable.
type SpiceNetlist {
  title: String
  body: List<DeckStatement>
}
